#{[17592186088416 :question/worked-solution "The solution is $V(t)=4800e^{kt}$. Apply the value after three years:\n\n$$\n7200=4800e^{3k}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k=\\frac{\\ln(3/2)}3\\approx0.135155.\n$$\n\nThe continuous annual growth rate is $14\\%$, to the nearest percent."] [17592186088421 :question/worked-solution "The solution with the given initial price is\n\n$$\nP(t)=250000e^{0.0325t}.\n$$\n\nAfter $25$ years,\n\n$$\nP(25)=250000e^{0.8125}\\approx563383.6968.\n$$\n\nRounded to the nearest dollar, the price is $\\$563384$."] [17592186088436 :question/worked-solution "Here $P(x)=2x$, so an integrating factor is\n$$\nI(x)=e^{\\int 2x\\,\\mathrm{d}x}=e^{x^2}.\n$$\nMultiplication by $I(x)$ gives the exact derivative\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(ye^{x^2})=e^{x^2}\\cdot 2xe^{-x^2}.\n$$\nThe product rule verifies the left side is $e^{x^2}(y'+2xy)$."] [17592186088444 :question/worked-solution "Set $z=y^2$, so $z^{\\prime}=2yy^{\\prime}$. Multiplying the ODE by $2y$ gives\n\n$$\nz^{\\prime}=x+\\frac{3z}{x}.\n$$\n\nOn an interval with $x\\ne0$, multiply $z^{\\prime}-3z/x=x$ by $x^{-3}$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dx}(zx^{-3})=x^{-2}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating gives $zx^{-3}=-x^{-1}+K$, hence\n\n$$\ny^2=Kx^3-x^2.\n$$\n\nThe original ODE requires $x\\ne0$ and $y\\ne0$. This implicit expression covers both sign branches on intervals where $Kx^3-x^2>0$."] [17592186088859 :tutorial/content "Recall that a first-order linear ODE can always be expressed in the standard form\n\n$$\ny'(x)+P(x)y(x) =Q(x).\n$$\n\nFirst-order linear ODEs are special because we can always find the general solution using an **integrating factor.** \n\nAn integrating factor is a function $I(x)$ that allows us to rewrite the left-hand side as an exact derivative after multiplying the equation by $I(x).$ The resulting equation can then be solved by integrating both sides.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the following first-order linear ODE.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + \\frac{2}{x}\\, y = 3\n$$\n\nAs it stands, this differential equation cannot be integrated directly. However, if we multiply both sides of the equation by $I(x) = x^2,$ we get\n\n$$\nx^2\\cdot \\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + x^2\\cdot \\frac{2}{x}\\, y = x^2\\cdot 3\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as follows:\n\n$$\nx^2 \\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + 2 x y = 3x^2 \\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nNow, here's the neat part. Provided our integrating factor is correct, we can *always* write the left-hand side of the equation as the exact derivative\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}\\left(y\\cdot I(x)\\right)\n$$\n\nwhich in our case is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}\\left(y \\cdot x^2\\right).\n$$\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}( y \\cdot x^2) = 3x^2.\n$$\n\nWe can now find the general solution by integrating both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot {x}^{2}) & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot {x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y\\cdot {x}^{2} & ={x}^{3}+C \\\\ y=x+\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can check that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}( y \\cdot x^2) = 3x^2\n$$\n\nis indeed equivalent to the original equation $(\\ast)$ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot {x}^{2}) & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)\\cdot {x}^{2}+y\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\cdot 2x & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy & =3{x}^{2}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy finding an appropriate integrating factor, we can solve any first-order linear ODE! So the question is, how do we find an integrating factor? Let's discuss this next."] [17592186088902 :tutorial/content "The equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $\\color{red}a$ and a vertical radius of length $\\color{blue}b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{{\\color{red}a}^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{{\\color{blue}b}^2}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\n![](images/92/9227839bbf9a01312c3014d336a88176eecd73aa934fcd4741c11271b1e0bea6.png)\n\nTo demonstrate, let's determine the equation of the following ellipse. The horizontal radius has length $\\color{red}a=3$ and the vertical radius has length $\\color{blue}b=2.$\n\n![](images/fe/fe7dff90438e9571ae599a446e355776fbd36ed4746dd76e6633f3848e1f6bbc.png)\n\nTherefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{{\\color{red}3}}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{{\\color{blue}2}}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186089243 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186089246] [17592186090604 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186090607] [17592186090610 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186090612] [17592186092512 :question/worked-solution "The chain rule gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm df}{\\mathrm dx}=e^{3x}\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dx}(3x)=3e^{3x}.\n$$"] [17592186092734 :question/worked-solution "Differentiate the outer exponential and multiply by the derivative of its exponent:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx}=e^{x^4+1}\\cdot4x^3=4x^3e^{x^4+1}.\n$$"] [17592186092735 :question/worked-solution "For a constant base, $\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dx}a^{u(x)}=a^{u(x)}\\ln(a)u^{\\prime}(x)$. With $u(x)=6x^2-4$,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx}=3^{6x^2-4}\\ln(3)\\cdot12x.\n$$"] [17592186093806 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186093807] [17592186094093 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186094096] [17592186094567 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186094572] [17592186094681 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186094684] [17592186094897 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186094900] [17592186094898 :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}x-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}x"] [17592186094899 :answer/value "2x\\operatorname{cos}x-2x\\operatorname{sin}x"] [17592186094900 :answer/value "2x\\operatorname{cos}x-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}x"] [17592186094901 :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}x-2x\\operatorname{sin}x"] [17592186094902 :answer/value "2x\\operatorname{cos}x+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}x"] [17592186094945 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186094949] [17592186094947 :answer/value "\\frac{5}{x}+\\frac{5\\operatorname{ln}x}{x\\operatorname{ln}3}"] [17592186095249 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186095253] [17592186095250 :answer/value "\\frac{{2}^{6x-1}}{\\operatorname{ln}2}+C"] [17592186095253 :answer/value "\\frac{{2}^{6x-1}}{6\\operatorname{ln}2}+C"] [17592186095254 :answer/value "\\frac{{2}^{3x-1}}{\\operatorname{ln}2}+C"] [17592186095255 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186095257] [17592186095257 :answer/value "-\\frac{{3}^{-x-6}}{\\operatorname{ln}3}+C"]}
